 ## Overview
-Linear Edge Intersection calculates the exact position where an isosurface (defined by a scalar field threshold) intersects a straight edge connecting two grid vertices, assuming the scalar field varies linearly between those vertices [S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. It solves the geometric problem of locating zero-crossings (or threshold crossings) on discrete grid structures, which is a fundamental step in algorithms like Marching Cubes and ray-marching volume rendering [S3, S6, unofficial]. Unlike [Voxel selection via pick ray intersection](893-voxel-selection-via-pick-ray-intersection.md), which determines which voxel a ray enters, this method determines where on a specific segment the surface lies [S6, unofficial]. It is distinguished from [Linear Intersection Approximation](1232-linear-intersection-approximation.md) by using the actual scalar values at the endpoints rather than assuming the midpoint.
